; examples/s0-heartbeat/system.eadl — the S0 base fixture.
;
; ROADMAP.md §12 S0 asks for "a roughly twenty-line feature-only description" whose generated
; hosted executable "instantiates one simple service and produces a defined event". This is it:
; one output device, one service, one platform, two periodic tasks — sixteen lines of
; declarations.
;
; WHY TWO TASKS AND NOT ONE. The observation horizon is the system's hyperperiod, and with a
; single task that is one period, so the trace is one event and no parameter change can produce
; a meaningfully different observation. Two is the smallest task set for which the S0 gate's
; changed-input case can say anything.
;
; WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. No execution bound, no entry point, no stack size — §7.3 puts
; those in the build manifest and the engine. Also no message text and no run length: the event
; format and the decision to observe exactly one hyperperiod are the engine's, and writing them
; here would be "embedding a prebuilt output without consuming the functional input", which §12
; S0 states does not satisfy F28.
;
; The expected observation is frozen in expected/system.txt and was written before any emitter
; existed. The observation contract it rests on is in README.md.

(defblock console.uart
  (offers (observable-output true)))

(defplatform host.playground
  (offers (core-count 1 tick))
  (requires (uses console.uart)))

(defservice console.write
  (requires (needs observable-output)))

(defsystem heartbeat
  (task beat
    (period 10 ms) (deadline 10 ms) (deadline-from release)
    (priority 1) (uses console.write) (on-overrun fault))
  (task chime
    (period 30 ms) (deadline 30 ms) (deadline-from release)
    (priority 2) (uses console.write) (on-overrun fault))
  (requires (uses console.write))
  (platform (uses host.playground)))
